  "query_preview": "..Clear skies were across most of southeast Arizona \nearly this morning, with just a few lower clouds noted northwest of \nTucson and in eastern Cochise County. Dewpoints were in the lower to \nmid 40s and satellite layer blended PW values were in the 0.30-0.45 \ninch range from Tucson eastward and between 0.50-0.60 inches over \ncentral/western Pima County.  \n\nAn upper low over far southeastern California will move into into \nwestern Arizona early this morning and then track northeast across \nthe state into southern Utah late tonight. This system was currently \ngenerating scattered showers over portions of southern California \nand the northern Baja Peninsula, with PW values near 0.75 inches in \nthose areas. As this system tracks northeast across the state today, \nthere may be just enough moisture combined with ascent into the \nhigher terrain of northern Graham and Greenlee counties for a slight \nchance of showers and thunderstorms. However, most of the forecast \narea will remain dry today, although I would not be surprised to see \na few light showers develop near the upper low's core especially \nduring peak daytime heating just to the north of our forecast area.  \n\nBehind the upper low, shortwave ridging and southwest flow aloft \nwill prevail Monday through Wednesday of next week, as a series of \nupper lows move into the Pacific Northwest. This will result in dry \nand warmer conditions areawide across southeast Arizona. \n\nDuring the latter half of next week the deterministic GFS/ECMWF and \nensembles are coming in consensus with west coast troughing and a \nmore active southern stream of moisture moving into the west coast. \nThere are still plenty of timing differences this far out. The \ncurrent forecast has high temperatures lowering below normal by the \nend of next week, as well as a slight chance of showers and \nthunderstorms Thursday and then a chance of showers/thunderstorms on \nFriday and Saturday.  \n\nOverall, high temperatures will be 2-5 degrees below normal through \nSunday, near to slightly above normal next Monday through Thursday, \nlowering to 4-8 degrees below normal at the end of next week into \nthe following weekend.",
